{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And if one man's ox...</b> — <b>shall strike.</b> Push — whether with its horns, whether with its body, whether with its leg, whether it bit with its teeth — all are included in נְגִיפָה, for נְגִיפָה is nothing but a term for a blow." "<b>The ox of one man.</b> An ox belonging to a man." "<b>And they shall sell the live ox, etc.</b> Scripture speaks of equal value — an ox worth two hundred that killed an ox worth two hundred — whether the carcass is worth a lot, whether it is worth little: when this one takes half the live and half the dead, and this one takes half the live and half the dead, it turns out each one loses half the damage that the killing caused. This teaches that the tam pays half-damage — for from the case of equal value you learn the case of unequal value, since the law of the tam is to pay half-damage — no less and no more. Or might one say that even for unequal value in their price while alive, Scripture said ״they shall divide the two of them״? If you say so, sometimes the damager profits a lot — when the carcass is worth a lot to sell to gentiles, more than the price of the damaging ox — and it is impossible for Scripture to say that the damager should profit. Or sometimes the injured party takes a lot more than the damage payment — when half the price of the damaging ox is worth more than the entire price of the injured ox — and if you say so, the tam is more stringent than the muad. Perforce, Scripture spoke only of equal value, and it teaches you that the tam pays half-damage, and from the equal case you learn the unequal: the one whose half-damage is paid — they appraise the carcass for him, and however much its value dropped because of the death, he takes half of the depreciation. And why did Scripture speak in this language and not say ״he shall pay half its value״? To teach that the tam pays only from its body, and if it gored and died, the injured party takes only the carcass — and if it does not reach half his damage, he loses; e.g., an ox worth a maneh that gored an ox worth five hundred zuz — he takes only the ox, for the tam was not held liable to obligate its owner to pay from above (Bava Kamma 33)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:50.087423993Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}